Creating a Kubernetes cluster is a solved problem. What comes after it is not:
delivery, secrets, certificates, isolation between tenants, observability,
backups. That is where the week goes.

So the second cluster is a repository you fork:
[hcloud-k3s-platform-kit](https://github.com/sasin91/hcloud-k3s-platform-kit).
Every number below was measured over four rounds of building and tearing down a
live cluster on Hetzner Cloud: three nodes in Falkenstein, one cpx22 control
plane and two cpx32 agents, behind an lb11 load balancer.

::: note
**Part 1 of 2 of [Where to start with
self-hosting](/blog/running-things-on-your-own-hardware/).** Next: [What a game
world server actually needs](/blog/what-a-game-world-server-needs/)
:::

## What it is

- **OpenTofu** for the machines, with
  [terraform-hcloud-kube-hetzner](https://github.com/mysticaltech/terraform-hcloud-kube-hetzner)
  as a pinned dependency. Not a fork of it. That project solves cluster creation
  itself, and none of it is rewritten here.
- **Flux** for delivery, with the controller manifests committed rather than
  bootstrapped. No personal access token, and nothing gets written back to your
  repo.
- **SOPS and age** for secrets. Exactly one secret in the whole system is
  applied by hand and cannot be encrypted.
- **Gateway API** for routing, where the platform owns its Gateway and tenants
  own their Routes.
- **VictoriaMetrics, VictoriaLogs and Tempo** behind an OpenTelemetry collector.
  The collector is what makes traces worth having, because it stamps
  `k8s.pod.name`, `k8s.namespace.name` and `k8s.node.name` onto every span. An
  application knows what it is. It does not know where the scheduler put it.
- **A namespace per tenant**, generated with its quota, its limits, its network
  policy, its disruption budget, its backup job, its pod security level and its
  RBAC.

No example tenant ships with it, and that is deliberate. A workload that just
occupies a namespace does not show anything the template does not already say.

## The price, measured

The same three nodes and load balancer, priced. The amounts include 25% Danish
VAT.

| Item | Type | €/month |
|---|---|---|
| Control plane × 1 | cpx22, 2 vCPU / 4 GB | 24.36 |
| Agents × 2 | cpx32, 4 vCPU / 8 GB | 88.72 |
| Load balancer | lb11 | 9.36 |
| **Total** | | **122.45** |

The kit's default shape is three control planes and two agents, so there is real
quorum. That is **€171.17**, before the autoscaler adds a machine.

A managed control plane from the large providers costs around €70 to €75 a month
on its own, before the first worker. But their workers cost several times
Hetzner's for the same specification, so you are not saving money by avoiding
managed Kubernetes at this size. You are saving it by being on Hetzner at all.
Those are two different decisions, and they get mixed up constantly.

## What it uses before your code runs

The cluster at rest, with one static site running.

| Namespace | Pods | CPU | Memory |
|---|---|---|---|
| observability | 19 | 141m | 2,858 Mi |
| flux-system | 4 | 9m | 596 Mi |
| kube-system | 11 | 33m | 580 Mi |
| cert-manager | 6 | 6m | 286 Mi |
| traefik | 2 | 4m | 150 Mi |
| system-upgrade | 1 | 1m | 56 Mi |
| **platform total** | 43 | **194m** | **4,526 Mi** |
| **the actual application** | 2 | 2m | **8 Mi** |

CPU is a rounding error: 194m out of 9,400m allocatable, about 2%. Memory is
what you are paying for, and two thirds of it goes to observability. The
overhead is 566 times the workload I measured it carrying.

That is also why the shape makes sense with several tenants and not with one.
The same 4.5 GiB carries both the second tenant and the tenth. Size the machines
for the platform first, and count on about 5 GiB before anything of your own
runs.

One number is worth knowing before you scale out. The cluster carries **161,507
active time series** at this size, and two histograms from the control plane,
`apiserver_request_duration_seconds_bucket` and
`etcd_request_duration_seconds_bucket`, account for 27% of them. Nobody queries
them. That is the default in every Kubernetes monitoring stack I have used.
Dropping those buckets or aggregating them is the cheapest win there is, and the
kit lets you make the decision instead of making it quietly for you.

## Where k3s is genuinely stronger

![Three options across a spectrum, three FreeBSD boxes, Docker Swarm, and the k3s platform kit, beneath two axes running in opposite directions: components acting on your behalf increase to the right while things you must write yourself decrease, and Swarm sits where they cross.](/images/k3s-platform-kit/spectrum.svg)

I also run [a production app on one FreeBSD
machine](/blog/athletos-freebsd/) for about €7 a month, and I have done the
arithmetic on doing the same with three. Put those machines on the same instance
type and the price difference disappears: three cpx32 is €133.08, more than the
cluster, because the cluster's control plane is a smaller machine. **The saving
is not in the operating system. It is that the FreeBSD machines fit in 4 GB and
the k3s machines do not.** So the cost argument against Kubernetes is really an
observability argument in disguise. If you would build metrics, logs and traces
anyway, you have already paid.

Docker Swarm sits in the middle, and I think it is under-rated. It is a mode of
the daemon you already run, `docker stack deploy` takes a compose file, and you
get scheduling, rolling updates, rollback, overlay networking, secrets in an
encrypted raft log and mutual TLS between machines. All of it in about a dozen
commands. Engine 29.7.1 came out in July 2026 with Swarm fixes throughout the
series, so "Swarm is dead" is almost always the removed *Classic* Swarm being
reported as the live one.

What Swarm has no answer for is the namespace: no admission step, no extension
mechanism, no per-tenant budget or policy layer.

And that is exactly what k3s wins on. I once measured guarantees across a mature
multi-tenant cluster. The guarantees issued by the path that creates a tenant,
meaning quota, limits, network policy, disruption budget, pod security level and
RBAC, were present in **over ninety percent** of namespaces. The same guarantees,
written down as recommendations, were present in **none**. Not in fewer. In
none.

The axis is not enforcement versus recommendation. It is in the generator versus
not in the generator, and Kubernetes is an unusually good target for a generator,
because every guarantee is an object with a name, a namespace and a schema, and
something checks it at the door. You can certainly build a jail generator, since
`jail.conf` is text, and that is a weekend rather than a research project. The
hard part to reproduce is a check that finds what was there before the
generator, and a refusal that happens at runtime rather than in CI.

So: under about ten services with one person authoring the isolation, the simple
option wins on every axis that matters. What flips it is the second person. One
person holds the invariants in their head and they are correct. Two people hold
two slightly different versions, and the difference turns up months later as one
customer seeing another customer's data.

## What is proven, and what is not

I have a hard time with "production-ready", so here is the line I will actually
defend.

Verified on that Hetzner Cloud cluster: a control plane of three nodes forms
with etcd;
the whole reconciliation chain converges from git with no intervention, 9 of 9
kustomizations and 8 of 8 Helm releases; a secret committed encrypted is
decrypted inside the cluster and consumed; a generated tenant serves public
traffic on a Let's Encrypt certificate the system trust store accepts; traces
arrive with pod, namespace and node on them; and the hostname admission policy
refuses another tenant's hostname, and refuses a route that declares none.

Not verified: **a restore.** The backup job is generated alongside every tenant
and has never been restored from. **The autoscaler under real pressure.** The
test project's quota left no room. **A control plane surviving the loss of a
member.** The three formed and were healthy, but I never killed one to watch the
other two carry on.

That second list is the honest part. I would rather write it down than have you
find it first.

## The part I would keep

A good model can produce something like this kit in an afternoon, and most of it
will work. The shapes are conventional and the tools are the obvious ones. What
it does not produce is the list of things that report success.

Every machine healthy, SSH working, and the orchestrator not running, because
Git on Windows had rewritten a file to CRLF inside a module the build tool
cloned on my behalf, where my `.gitattributes` had no say. The SELinux policy,
compiled on the machine itself, rejected the carriage return, and the only
visible symptom was an opaque provisioner error two layers further up.

Or `tofu destroy` reporting success while a machine created by the autoscaler
kept running and kept costing money, because destroy only walks its own state,
and the cluster creates things at runtime that never entered it. I had written
"destroy is clean, zero residue" in the README. That was true and useless,
because those runs had never autoscaled anything.

Forty-nine of that kind are written down in the repository, each stated as a
mechanism with the failure it produced. That file is the part I would keep if I
had to throw the rest away.
